L399: ##### Selected Ablations
L400: In cite194†Table 8 we report a subset of the more extensive ablations from §cite42†A . Note that the ablated models are from different, shorter training runs than the model evaluated above. The presence head boosts cgF_{1} by +1.5 (), improving image-level recognition measured by IL_MCC by +0.05. shows that adding hard negatives significantly improves the model performance, most notably the image-level IL_MCC from 0.44 to 0.68.
L401: shows that synthetic (SYN) training data improves over the external (EXT) by +8.8 cgF_{1} and our high-quality (HQ) annotations add +14.6 cgF_{1} on top of this baseline. We present detailed data scaling laws of both types of data in §cite46†A.2 , showing their effectiveness on both in-domain and out-of-domain test sets. In , we show how AI verifiers can improve pseudo-labels. Replacing the presence score from SAM 3 with that score from the exhaustivity verification (EV) AI verifier boosts cgF_{1} by +7.2.
L402: Using the mask verification (MV) AI verifier to remove bad masks adds another 1.1 points. Overall, AI verifiers close half of the gap between SAM 3’s and human performance.
L403: Figure 9: Domain adaptation via synthetic data. Synthetic (SYN) data generated by SAM 3 + AI verifiers (teacher system) achieves similar scaling behavior as human-annotated (HQ) data.
L404: ##### Domain adaptation ablation
L405: With domain-specific synthetic data generated by SAM 3 + AI verifiers, we show that one can significantly improve performance on a new domain without any human annotation. We hold out one of the SA-Co domains, “Food&drink”, from training SAM 3 and AI verifiers.
L406: We then use three variants of training data for the novel “Food&drink” domain: high-quality AI+human annotations as in SA-Co/HQ (referred to as SA-Co/HQ-Food), synthetic annotations as in SA-Co/SYN, using AI but no humans (SA-Co/SYN-Food), and pseudo-labels generated before the AI verification step, i.e. skipping both AI verifiers and humans (PL-Food). Fig. cite195†9 plots performance on the “Food&drink” test set of the SA-Co/Gold benchmark as each type of training data is scaled up.
L407: We mix the domain specific data and high-quality general domain data at a 1:1 ratio. PL-Food provides some improvement compared to the baseline SAM 3 (zero-shot), but is far below the other variants due to its lower quality. HQ-Food and SYN-Food show similar scaling behavior, with SYN-Food slightly lower but eventually catching up, without incurring any human annotation cost. This points to a scalable way to improve performance on new data distributions. More details are in §cite52†A.3 .
L408: ## 7 Related Work
L409: Promptable and Interactive Visual Segmentation. SAM (cite156†Kirillov et al., 2023 ) introduces “promptable” image segmentation with interactive refinement. While the original task definition included text prompts, they were not fully developed. SAM 2 (cite157†Ravi et al., 2024 ) extended the promptable visual segmentation task to video, allowing refinement points on any frame.
L410: SAM 3 inherits geometry-based segmentation while extending to include text and image exemplar prompts to segment all instances of a concept in images and videos.
L411: Open-Vocabulary Detection and Segmentation in Images exhaustively labels every instance of an open-vocabulary object category with a coarse bounding box (detection) or a fine-grained pixel mask (segmentation).
L412: Recent open-vocabulary (OV) detection  (cite196†Gu et al., 2021 ; cite197†Minderer et al., 2022 ) and segmentation (cite198†Ding et al., 2022 ; cite199†Liang et al., 2023 ) methods leverage large-scale vision-language encoders such as CLIP (cite200†Radford et al., 2021 ) to handle categories described by arbitrary text, even those never seen during training.
L413: While DETR (cite162†Carion et al., 2020 ) is limited to a closed set of categories seen during training, MDETR (cite167†Kamath et al., 2021 ) evolves the approach to condition on raw text queries. Image exemplars used as prompts to specify the desired object category (e.g., DINOv (cite201†Li et al., 2023a ), T-Rex2 (cite202†Jiang et al., 2024 )) present a practical alternative to text, but fall short in conveying the abstract concept of objects as effectively as text prompts.
L414: We introduce a new benchmark for OV segmentation with $>100\times$ more unique concepts than prior work.
L415: Visual Grounding localizes a language expression referring to a region of the image with a box or mask. (cite203†Plummer et al., 2020 ) introduces phrase detection as both deciding whether the phrase is relevant to an image and localizing it. GLIP (cite204†Li et al., 2022b ) and GroundingDino (cite205†Liu et al., 2023 ) formulate object detection as phrase grounding, unifying both tasks during training. MQ-GLIP (cite206†Xu et al., 2023 ) adds image exemplars to text as queries.
L416: Building on this trend toward models supporting multiple tasks and modalities, GLEE (cite207†Wu et al., 2024a ) allows text phrases, referring expressions, and visual prompts for category and instance grounding in both images and videos. Unlike SAM 3, GLEE does not support exemplars or interactive refinement.
L417: LISA (cite208†Lai et al., 2024 ) allows segmentation that requires reasoning, while OMG-LLaVa (cite209†Zhang et al., 2024a ) and GLaMM (cite210†Rasheed et al., 2024 ) generate natural language responses interleaved with corresponding segmentation masks, with GLaMM accepting both textual and optional image prompts as input. Some general-purpose MLLMs can output boxes and masks (Gemini2.5 (cite211†Comanici et al., 2025 )) or points (Molmo (cite212†Deitke et al., 2025 )).
L418: SAM 3 can be used as a “vision tool” in combination with an MLLM (§cite36†6 ).
L419: Multi-Object Tracking and Segmentation methods identify object instances in video and track them, associating each with a unique ID.
L420: In tracking-by-detection methods, detection is performed independently on each frame to produce boxes and confidence scores, followed by association of boxes using motion-based and appearance-based matching as in SORT (cite213†Bewley et al., 2016 ; cite214†Wojke et al., 2017 ), Tracktor (cite215†Bergmann et al., 2019 ), ByteTrack (cite216†Zhang et al., 2022c ), SAM2MOT (cite217†Jiang et al., 2025 ), or OC-SORT (cite218†Cao et al., 2023 ).
L421: An alternative is an end-to-end trainable architecture that jointly detects and associates objects, e.g., TrackFormer (cite219†Meinhardt et al., 2022 ), TransTrack (cite220†Sun et al., 2020 ), or MOTR (cite221†Zeng et al., 2022 ). TrackFormer uses a DETR-like encoder-decoder that initializes new tracks from static object queries and auto-regressively follows existing tracks with identity-preserving track queries.
L422: A challenge with joint models is the conflict between detection and tracking  (cite222†Feichtenhofer et al., 2017 ; cite223†Yu et al., 2023a ), where one needs to focus on semantics while the other on disentangling identities, even if their spatial locations overlap over time. SAM 3 is a strong image detector tightly integrated into a tracker to segment concepts in videos.
L423: ## 8 Conclusion

